# Copy this file to `.env` for local development and fill in real values.
#   Copy-Item .env.example .env
#
# NEVER commit `.env` — it is git-ignored. Never put database credentials, API
# keys, or privileged tokens in frontend code or in VITE_* variables: anything
# prefixed with VITE_ is embedded in the browser bundle.
#
# The keys below are the complete set the application actually reads, with where
# each one comes from in a deployed environment:
# docs/azure-architecture.md section 5 (configuration contract),
# docs/azure-deployment.md (Azure runbook) and docs/render-staging.md (Render
# staging runbook).

# --- Application ------------------------------------------------------------
# development | test | staging | production
# `staging` and `production` are the *protected* environments: the development
# sign-in route is not registered, the development placeholder secret is
# stripped, and authentication must come from a real configuration below.
ENVIRONMENT=development

# --- Authentication & authorization (Phase 7, ADR-0014; Phase 6.0, ADR-0025) -
# DataDiet stores NO passwords. There is a directory of accounts, and a bearer
# token proves which one is calling. Authorization always comes from the
# directory — never from token claims, and never from identity-provider metadata.
#
# Two verification paths exist, and which one is live is decided by configuration:
#
#   1. Clerk (ADR-0025): session tokens are RS256 and are verified against
#      Clerk's published JWKS. This is the staging/production path.
#   2. The legacy bearer boundary (ADR-0014): an external signer issues HS256
#      tokens with AUTH_JWT_SECRET and this API only verifies them. It stays
#      available in a protected environment when CLERK_ISSUER is not configured
#      and a real (non-placeholder) AUTH_JWT_SECRET is set.
#
# A protected environment with neither fails closed: every authenticated
# endpoint returns 503 `auth_not_configured`, while /health and /health/ready
# stay reachable so a platform can tell misconfiguration from a dead service.
#
# Leave AUTH_JWT_SECRET EMPTY in development: a well-known development value is
# used automatically and `POST /api/v1/auth/dev-login` signs synthetic accounts
# in. Setting CLERK_ISSUER in development also disables dev-login, because the
# tokens it mints could not be verified by the Clerk path.
#
# In ENVIRONMENT=staging|production you MUST configure Clerk or set a real
# AUTH_JWT_SECRET (>= 32 characters). Generate one with:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
# AUTH_JWT_SECRET=
# Issuer/audience for the HS256 bearer boundary; must match whoever signs.
AUTH_JWT_ISSUER=datadiet-dev
AUTH_JWT_AUDIENCE=datadiet-api
# Token lifetime in seconds (60..86400). Applies to tokens this API signs
# itself (development); Clerk controls the lifetime of its own session tokens.
AUTH_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=3600
# Development/test sign-in only. Always forced false in a protected environment.
AUTH_DEV_PROVIDER_ENABLED=true

# --- Clerk (Phase 6.0, ADR-0025) --------------------------------------------
# Setting CLERK_ISSUER is what turns Clerk verification on; there is no implicit
# default. It is the `iss` value Clerk writes into session tokens, i.e. the
# instance's frontend API origin, e.g. https://institution-12.clerk.accounts.dev
# Must be HTTPS (loopback is allowed only for local fixtures) or the protected
# environment refuses to start.
# CLERK_ISSUER=
# The JWKS endpoint. Derived from the issuer as `<issuer>/.well-known/jwks.json`
# when empty, which is the shape Clerk documents. Keys are cached, so this is not
# fetched on every request.
# CLERK_JWKS_URL=
# Set when the Clerk JWT template names an API instance (`aud`); the value must
# match the template exactly. Empty means "this template carries no audience".
# CLERK_AUDIENCE=
# Whether an unknown Clerk subject may be given a *permissionless* account
# automatically. Off by default: an unrecognised `sub` is refused (401) and an
# administrator binds it to an account explicitly
# (scripts/bind_clerk_identity.py). Even when on, provisioning never grants
# platform administration or an institution membership.
CLERK_AUTO_PROVISION=false
# NOTE: there is deliberately no CLERK_SECRET_KEY. This API is a resource server:
# it verifies tokens and reads its own directory. No server-side Clerk API is
# used, so no Clerk secret key is read, stored, or needed.

# --- Backend API ------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT READ BY THE APPLICATION. uvicorn is started with an explicit `--port`
# (see docs/setup.md) and the FastAPI app binds nothing from these names; they
# are kept here only as a reminder of the conventional local endpoints.
API_HOST=127.0.0.1
API_PORT=8000

# Allowed browser origins, as a comma-separated list OR a JSON array:
#   CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.example.edu,https://admin.example.edu
#   CORS_ORIGINS=["https://app.example.edu","https://admin.example.edu"]
# The comma form is the portable one: deployed platforms (App Service
# application settings, for instance) can only carry flat strings. Empty/unset
# falls back to the frontend dev server below. In production list explicit
# HTTPS origins only — never `*` with credentials, and never a `http://` origin.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# Phase 1: serve synthetic demo endpoints (/api/v1/demo/*). Set to false to
# disable them — they are not real data, but they should not exist in production.
DEMO_DATA_ENABLED=true

# --- Phase 4: controlled data ingestion -------------------------------------
# Allow-listed local directories a `local_folder` source may scan, as a JSON
# object of key -> absolute path (JSON only — unlike CORS_ORIGINS there is no
# comma form for a dict field). The API only accepts a KEY, never a path, and
# resolves it here. Empty by default: local-folder sources stay disabled until
# an operator opts in. Never scan a whole drive, home directory, or the repo.
#   LOCAL_SOURCE_ROOTS={"dev-fixtures":"C:/Users/you/datadiet-fixtures"}
LOCAL_SOURCE_ROOTS={}
# Hard ceiling on records a single scan may ingest (bounds work per scan).
SCAN_MAX_RECORDS=5000
# Default discovery batch size when a scan does not override it.
SCAN_BATCH_SIZE=500

# --- Phase 6.x: object storage provider (ADR-0026) ---------------------------
# An *isolated* capability: the provider and its configuration are implemented
# and tested, but no DataDiet workflow calls it yet — nothing is uploaded or
# downloaded on any request path. Keep it that way until a real consumer is
# designed (see ADR-0026 in docs/decisions.md); `STORAGE_PROVIDER=none` is the
# default precisely so that installing the SDK cannot quietly turn on a code
# path nobody exercised.
#
# DataDiet stays metadata-only: PostgreSQL remains the application database and
# the store holds only object bytes. Authorization is never decided by anything
# in this section.
#
# Recognised values: `none` (default — no store is built and the Azure SDK is
# not even imported) and `azure_blob`.
STORAGE_PROVIDER=none
# Connection string for the chosen account. Any value here is a secret:
# it carries the account key, so it belongs in the environment only — never in
# a VITE_* variable, never in source, and never in a log line.
#
# There is deliberately no separate AZURE_STORAGE_ENDPOINT variable. The
# connection string already names the endpoint (BlobEndpoint=...), and a second
# source of truth for one endpoint is how a request ends up signed for one
# account and sent to another.
#
# Local development against the Floci Azure emulator, bound to loopback on
# `http://127.0.0.1:4577` (see ADR-0026 for what was measured on that emulator).
# `devstoreaccount1` and its key are the emulator's fixed well-known identity,
# not a real credential — read the current value from the emulator itself rather
# than copying one from a screenshot:
#   docker exec floci-az sh -c 'env | grep -i -E "account|key"'
# or `floci az env`. The key must match the one the running emulator uses; two
# different documented values have been observed.
#   AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=DefaultEndpointsProtocol=http;AccountName=devstoreaccount1;AccountKey=<emulator-account-key>;BlobEndpoint=http://127.0.0.1:4577/devstoreaccount1;
# AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=
# Container that holds every DataDiet object. Must be a valid Azure blob
# container name (3-63 lowercase alphanumeric/hyphen, no consecutive hyphens).
# One container for the whole deployment; object keys are namespaced in the
# application, not split across containers.
AZURE_STORAGE_CONTAINER=datadiet
# DataDiet refuses to build a provider whose blob endpoint is not HTTPS unless
# this is explicitly on. (It has to check itself: the SDK's advertised
# `require_https` keyword is swallowed by `**kwargs` and enforces nothing in
# azure-storage-blob 12.31.1.) The flag exists for the local emulator — plain
# HTTP on loopback — and for no other reason; a protected environment refuses
# to start with it enabled, because it would send the account key over a
# plaintext transport.
#
# NOTE about the emulator: Floci does *not* reliably enforce SharedKey
# authentication — unsigned and forged-signature requests have been observed to
# succeed against it. Never treat emulator-side authentication as a security
# boundary; passing local tests says the wire protocol works, not that
# credentials are verified.
AZURE_STORAGE_ALLOW_HTTP=false
# Per-operation timeout in seconds (1..300) for the SDK's own HTTP client.
AZURE_STORAGE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# SQLAlchemy URL. Development default matches infra/docker-compose.dev.yml
# (MySQL); PostgreSQL is the staging target (ADR-0025).
#
# For PostgreSQL the driver suffix is REQUIRED, not decorative: SQLAlchemy
# resolves a bare `postgresql://` URL to the psycopg-3 driver, which is not
# installed, so the engine would fail at connect time with a confusing import
# error. Write `postgresql+psycopg2://`.
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg2://datadiet:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/datadiet_staging
#
# In a deployed environment this value is a secret and must come from the
# platform's secret store (on Render: an encrypted Environment Secret; on Azure:
# a Key Vault reference — never a literal in an application setting). The engine
# passes no TLS options of its own, so server-enforced TLS is configured here, in
# the URL's query string; see docs/azure-deployment.md section 6 and
# docs/render-staging.md before enabling require_secure_transport / sslmode.
DATABASE_URL=mysql+pymysql://datadiet:datadiet_dev_password@localhost:3306/datadiet
# Log SQL statements (development only; very noisy).
DATABASE_ECHO=false

# Values NOT read by the application: infra/docker-compose.dev.yml sets its own
# container environment inline. They are here only to document the development
# container's credentials, which DATABASE_URL above must match.
MYSQL_HOST=localhost
MYSQL_PORT=3306
MYSQL_DATABASE=datadiet
MYSQL_USER=datadiet
MYSQL_PASSWORD=datadiet_dev_password

# --- Frontend ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The only variables that reach the browser bundle are the VITE_ ones below.
# Anything prefixed VITE_ is public by definition, so a secret must never be
# given a VITE_ name.
# Vite reads this repository-root file (frontend/vite.config.ts sets
# `envDir: ".."`), so keep secrets out of the VITE_ namespace here.
# Leave EMPTY in development: the Vite dev server proxies /api to the backend,
# so no CORS setup is needed. In deployed environments set the public API origin,
# e.g. VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://datadiet-api.example.edu — it is baked in at
# build time, so changing it requires a rebuild and redeploy of the frontend.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=
# Clerk's *publishable* key (starts `pk_`), from the Clerk dashboard's "API keys"
# page. It is a browser value by design — it identifies which Clerk instance this
# build talks to, and grants nothing. Set it only where the backend is configured
# with the matching CLERK_ISSUER above; leave it empty to keep the frontend on
# DataDiet's own sign-in screen, which then reports the deployment's real
# authentication mode.
#
# The Clerk *secret* key (`sk_`) is a different credential and belongs nowhere in
# this file or in any VITE_ variable: this API verifies Clerk session tokens
# against the public JWKS and never calls Clerk's server-side API, so it needs no
# secret key at all. With this key set but no CLERK_ISSUER the frontend would show
# Clerk's form against an API that refuses Clerk tokens — configure both together.
# e.g. VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_Y2xlcmsuZXhhbXBsZS5vcmc
VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# --- Phase 2+ placeholders (NOT used yet) -----------------------------------
# These integrations do not exist yet. Do not add values until implemented.
# SMTP_HOST=
# NOTIFICATION_WEBHOOK_URL=
# OBJECT_STORAGE_BACKEND was an early placeholder for object storage. It is
# retired: Phase 6.x implemented that capability under its real variable names
# (STORAGE_PROVIDER and the AZURE_STORAGE_* block above), which the settings
# model actually reads. Nothing reads OBJECT_STORAGE_BACKEND.
